# npm 7 and later enforce peer dependency ranges strictly. This project is on
# React 19 but carries a Velzon template full of packages that predate it, so a
# plain `npm install` fails on a peer conflict.
#
# Six unused packages that caused conflicts have been removed - echarts,
# echarts-for-react, google-maps-react, react-credit-cards, react-image-lightbox
# and react-rangeslider - and that is NOT enough to drop this file. npm reports
# one conflict at a time, and behind those sit packages that are genuinely used:
#
#   react-dual-listbox peers on react ^16 || ^17 || ^18, and its stylesheet is
#   imported by src/assets/scss/themes.scss
#   react-table and react-dropzone are imported by application code
#
# Removing those is not an option, and chasing the rest one at a time is the
# wrong game: the real fix is replacing or forking the packages that stopped at
# React 18, which is a project rather than an install flag.
#
# So the flag stays, and it belongs here rather than in everyone's muscle
# memory: a fresh clone installs with `npm install`, and nobody has to discover
# the incantation partway through a deployment.
#
# Deliberately not --force. That tells npm to write a tree it knows is broken.
# --legacy-peer-deps restores npm 6 behaviour, where peer ranges are advisory
# and the packages installed are exactly what the lockfile says.
legacy-peer-deps=true
